#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157044 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157426 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da01b8a7-bbe2-57aa-931d-3fc1f54e4b9e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/id #uuid "35283781-7e25-5144-9bdd-77921cf5ea26" :question/math-academy-id "e-16703" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^2 + e^{-2x}$ in the interval $[0,1]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.5$. Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$, respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (x^{2} + e^{-2x})^{′} \\\\\n&= 2x - 2e^{-2x}, \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= (2x - 2e^{-2x})^{′} \\\\\n&= 2 + 4e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\nx_{n + 1} = x_{n} - \\frac{2x_{n} - 2e^{-2x_{n}}}{2 + 4e^{-2x_{n}}}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.5$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\frac{2x_{0} - 2e^{-2x_{0}}}{2 + 4e^{-2x_{0}}} \\\\\n&= 0.5 - (2(0.5) - 2e^{-2(0.5)})/(2 + 4e^{-2(0.5)}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.424\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157045 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/id #uuid "1bf35491-4dda-5bff-9a9e-c3e91f9d5edc" :question/math-academy-id "e-16704" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^3-6x^2+9x$ in the interval $[0,2]$. Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0$." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$, respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (x^{3} - 6x^{2} + 9x)^{′} = 3x^{2} - 12x + 9, \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= (3x^{2} - 12x + 9)^{′} = 6x - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - \\frac{3x_{n}^{2} - 12x_{n} + 9}{6x_{n} - 12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\frac{3x_{0}^{2} - 12x_{0} + 9}{6x_{0} - 12} \\\\\n&= 0 - (3(0)^{2} - 12(0) + 9)/(6(0) - 12) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{12} \\\\\n&= 0.75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} - \\frac{3x_{1}^{2} - 12x_{1} + 9}{6x_{1} - 12} \\\\\n&= 0.75 - (3(0.75)^{2} - 12(0.75) + 9)/(6(0.75) - 12) \\\\\n&= 0.975\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x_1 = 0.75$ and\n\n$$\nx_2 = 0.975\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157046 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/id #uuid "67b4f920-532d-56dc-8b6b-c76d5115959d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16705" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=x^4-3x+1$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,2]$, use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$, respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (x^{4} - 3x + 1)^{′} = 4x^{3} - 3, \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= (4x^{3} - 3)^{′} = 12x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - \\frac{4x_{n}^{3} - 3}{12x_{n}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (12x_{n}^{3} - (4x_{n}^{3} - 3))/(12x_{n}^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8x_{n}^{3} + 3}{12x_{n}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= \\frac{8x_{0}^{3} + 3}{12x_{0}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (8(1)^{3} + 3)/(12(1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 0.916666…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= \\frac{8x_{1}^{3} + 3}{12x_{1}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (8(0.916666…)^{3} + 3)/(12(0.916666…)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 0.908631…, \\\\\nx_{3} &= \\frac{8x_{2}^{3} + 3}{12x_{2}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (8(0.908631…)^{3} + 3)/(12(0.908631…)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 0.908560…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations\n\n$$\nx_2 \\approx0.91\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_3 \\approx 0.91\n$$\n\nare the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is\n\n$$\nx \\approx 0.91\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "784f0dbb-d926-5461-9f9f-98f165e27f6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154804}] :question/id #uuid "c86499a3-b11b-54e3-8a70-04bd4551cb72" :question/math-academy-id "q-256464" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x) = x^{3} + x^{2} - 3x + 4$ in the interval $[0, 2]$. Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point $x_{0} = 0$. Round your answer to three decimal places.$x_{2} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157426 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da01b8a7-bbe2-57aa-931d-3fc1f54e4b9e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "63671e40-590f-536b-8dfb-757855c8f75f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154807}] :question/id #uuid "bbd4e823-0309-5688-94b6-82a55c915b15" :question/math-academy-id "q-256810" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x) = \\sin (x^{2}) + x^{3}$ in the interval $[- 2, 0]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_{0} =-1$. Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.$x_{1} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}"}]}
